  <p><strong>Can my supplier substitute fabric on a repeat activewear order?</strong></p>

  <h2>Build an order reference you can retrieve</h2>
  <p>Attach the previous PO, style code, colour, size chart, approved fabric code and artwork version. Keep an approved garment in usable condition. A catalogue photo may help identify the style but cannot show the original hand feel or stretch recovery.</p>

  <h2>Compare the garment, not just the swatch</h2>
  <p>Two fabrics with the same fibre percentages and GSM can behave differently. For leggings, check coverage and waistband recovery. For a sports bra, check band tension and the fit of the relevant sizes. If a heat-transfer logo was used, repeat adhesion and wash checks on the substitute.</p>

  <h2>Document what stays interchangeable</h2>
  <p>If the new garment differs visibly or fits differently, decide whether it can share the original inventory and product listing. Record the updated spec, approval date and lot. Keep the stock check for an existing model separate from any request to develop a replacement.</p>

  <p>Industry reference: <a href="https://www.tillys.com/on/demandware.static/-/Library-Sites-AutobahnSharedLibrary/default/dwc97277a1/images/vendor/Tillys-Private-Label-Compliance-Manual-01-22-2024.pdf">Tillys’ vendor manual gives one buyer example of requiring renewed fit review after material or trim performance changes.</a></p>
